---
source: tests/snapshots_help_contracts.rs
expression: "help(&scratch, None, &[\"--help\", \"worktree\", \"prune\"])"
---
Usage: worktree prune [--repo PATH] [--stale-days N] [--release-stale --reason TEXT] [--dryrun] [--json|--format json] [--remove] [--no-sync]

Global, removal-by-default cleanup of managed worktrees, grouped by canonical directory. Without --repo it enumerates every repository identity the local lease store records (even outside Git) and resolves each through its main checkout, skipping an identity it cannot verify with a bounded reason instead of guessing; --repo PATH restricts the pass to one verified checkout. A directory is removed as soon as every issue associated with it is confirmed closed on the configured provider, no active lease references the canonical directory, and the shared candidate guards clear it (never the main checkout, never a path an `active` lease points at, never dirty or untracked, and never a directory the OpenCode host still — or cannot be proven not to — occupy, checked read-only); removal then uses `git worktree remove` without `--force` after a fresh re-check. A directory whose only remaining occupant is one or more individually proven associated child sessions may be cleaned after prune relocates exactly those children to the repository's verified main checkout — a child qualifies only when its `parentID` chain (at most 8 hops) reaches a persisted `ses_` lease session for this directory and it is not itself a lease owner; the child's execution outcome, whether succeeded, failed, interrupted, cancelled, absent, or unknown, is never a cleanup criterion — and only after a fresh read confirms each relocation and a fresh complete listing plus fresh lease rows re-prove vacancy and safety; a foreign, unreadable, parentless, cyclic, or over-deep session, an unavailable main checkout, or any failed or unconfirmed move keeps the directory, and prune never moves a lease owner or an unrelated session. There is no waiting period: a freshly retained closed clean directory is removed immediately, so --stale-days never gates directory deletion and exists only for the explicit --release-stale recovery below. Closure is read from the configured provider through the same current-configured-provider resolution the rest of the CLI uses; a missing, unavailable, ambiguous, or mismatched issue is never treated as closed, and a preview may read closure but never mutates. The local guards — never the main checkout, never a path an `active` lease points at, never dirty or untracked — are decided before any host or provider read, so a main-checkout or dirty directory is excluded without contacting the host. Reported relocations stay faithful: a removal pass lists exactly the children whose move it confirmed, even when a later occupancy check keeps the directory, while a preview lists only proposed relocations and never labels them actual, and the human failure count scopes to failed `git worktree remove` calls rather than to host-anomaly evidence. --dryrun (alias --dry-run) is the read-only preview: it performs the same read-only host discovery to report proposed relocations and eligibility, but never mutates any directory, lease row, or session and never moves a session. --release-stale requires a non-empty --reason unless it is a preview and flips exactly the active leases whose heartbeat is older than --stale-days to `retained` in a single transaction; a directory that held an active lease at invocation start is never removed by the same call, and --reason without --release-stale is rejected. The default stdout is concise English text with directory-based counts; --json or --format json selects the machine-readable aggregate envelope (per repository plus totals). --remove is an accepted compatibility no-op and --no-sync is accepted but no longer runs a sync pass: prune never syncs. Branches and lease rows are never deleted. A few `worktree prune:` progress lines stream to stderr, flushed before the potentially slow host and closure checks so a long scan is visibly working, while stdout stays reserved for the final text or the single JSON document; within one invocation the read-only scan shares a repeated host answer, but every removal re-checks the host live. Orchestrator-only.
